WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
WESLEY CHUN:
Greetings, developers,

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.480
and welcome to Serverless
Migration Station, where

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.300
we show you one serverless
computer modernization

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:06.840
technique.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.120
I'm Wesley Chun at
Google, joined by Martin

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.340
and our friend Porter here
to take you from point A

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.270
to point B. Today's migration
represents a product crossover

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.430
showing you how to
containerize your App Engine

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.340
apps to run on Cloud Run, our
serverless container hosting

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:20.970
service.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.340
MARTIN: Wow, that sounds
pretty complicated.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.330
Containerizing your app?

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.450
I'm just a simple web developer.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.880
How much do I have to
learn about containers?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.380
WESLEY CHUN: Great
question, Martin.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.930
Developers out there
already familiar with Docker

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.630
don't have much to learn since
they've already been creating

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.100
and deploying containers.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.320
Using Docker to create a
container for Cloud Run

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.510
is pretty much the
same as anywhere else.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.750
On the other hand, if you're
completely new to containers,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.790
don't worry, we'll
take care of you, too.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.340
In this module 4 video,
we'll focus on Docker.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.315
But in module 5,
we'll show you how

00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:54.690
to containerize your apps
without having to know anything

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.510
about Docker or containers.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.010
MARTIN: That's great to know
that Google Cloud is for Docker

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.890
veterans and container newbies.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.170
So for folks like
me who are new,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.243
what's the elevator pitch
for containers anyway?

00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:08.160
WESLEY CHUN: Well, I'm
glad you asked, Martin.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.470
With a seed planted
years ago, containers

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.420
came to the forefront
when virtualization

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.820
became a thing around the
beginning of the millennium,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.470
and were accelerated
specifically

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.770
for application packaging
about a decade later by Docker.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.290
Of course, programming has been
around for longer than that.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.420
And some of us have been
writing code for ages

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.590
without containers.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.900
While optional, containerizing
gives developers

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.810
the most freedom and flexibility
in bundling their app.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.960
They're easily reproducible,
and you can use or reuse them

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.990
anywhere you want, making
them extremely portable, too.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:37.770
MARTIN: Come on.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.230
Those are just marketing
buzzwords, Wes.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.870
What do they mean for
developers like us?

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.170
WESLEY CHUN: Well, you asked
for the elevator pitch.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.670
This is going to take a little
longer than one elevator ride

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:48.450
now.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.150
Well, containers give you the
flexibility to bundle whatever

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.060
your apps need, freeing you
from traditional serverless

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.430
restrictions like, you
have to use this language,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.970
or you can't muddle that
library because it's in C++.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.460
Furthermore, the
container is reproducible.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.308
You can figure how the
container is built.

00:02:04.308 --> 00:02:05.850
And if you use the
same instructions,

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.020
you could create an
identical container.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.199
This is valuable, not
just for testing, but also

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:12.060
for verifying your
app works with which

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.590
versions of your dependencies.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.560
No more deploying apps and
hoping the production libraries

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.660
are compatible.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.730
MARTIN: OK, so my users are
based all over the world.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.260
So I want to deploy the
same service everywhere.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.540
Also, if I had a
bad build, I want

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.110
to roll back to the
previous working version.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.510
So the same container image
can be deployed anywhere?

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.600
And if there's a
problem, I can roll back

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.107
to a previous container image?

00:02:38.107 --> 00:02:39.190
WESLEY CHUN: Oh, for sure.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.065
I mean, I know we're
talking serverless here.

00:02:41.065 --> 00:02:43.530
But realize containers
are ultra portable,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.260
as that same container image
can be deployed to your Compute

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.830
Engine VMs, Kubernetes, in
the cloud, your VMs on-prem,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.680
hybrid, or even multi-cloud.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.690
Think of containers
like a shopping bag

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.940
to bundle your code
and its dependencies.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.220
Imagine carrying everything
you bought at the market

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.140
separately to your
car or on the subway.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.450
MARTIN: [CHUCKLES] That
shopping-bag analogy

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.020
makes a lot of sense to me.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.290
I'm sold on containers.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.220
But do I have to containerize
my App Engine apps?

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.380
I'm kind of used to App Engine's
style of development, where

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.950
I improve my code, I
deploy to staging, and then

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.450
eventually to production.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.200
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, as
I mentioned earlier,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.570
I've been writing apps for
years without containers.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.840
So if you're comfortable
there, that's fine.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.660
But realize that industry
has moved towards containers

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.578
in a big way so they're there
for you when you're ready.

00:03:30.578 --> 00:03:32.370
I think you'll like
our next video, though,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.230
because we'll show you
how to containerize apps

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.820
without knowing much
about containers.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.350
So stay tuned for that.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.990
For now, Google has
expressed long-term support

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:40.830
for App Engine.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.900
But much of our effort
is going to Cloud Run

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.420
because containers are
a de facto standard now.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.370
MARTIN: Well, I'm ready now.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.720
But before that, I do have
some Python 2 legacy apps.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.570
I know it's been deprecated
by the community and all that.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.340
But so I'm concerned.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.867
Do you have any options for me?

00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:00.700
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah,
you're not alone, Martin.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.580
Many Python 2 developers
share those concerns.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.615
However, that long-term support
page links to another page

00:04:06.615 --> 00:04:09.910
where Google has committed to
supporting the App Engine 2.7

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.340
runtime, letting
you move when ready.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.350
And did you already
forget that Cloud Run

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:17.410
supports any language,
any library, any binary?

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:20.740
Containerizing is one way to
keep your Python 2 app alive

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.760
forever.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.650
MARTIN: Well, if you
put it that way, Wes,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.850
I'm going to containerize two
App Engine apps, the Python 2

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:33.130
Cloud NDB app from module 2 and
the Python 3 Cloud Datastore

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.867
app from module 3.

00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:35.950
WESLEY CHUN: Sure, Martin.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.020
Porter has lots of
energy today, so we'll

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.840
do both, which is why there
are Python 2 and 3 folders

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.580
for module 4 when we're done.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.460
Check out the module 2 or
3 videos and repo folders

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.710
to refamiliarize
ourselves with each.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.973
The Python 2 Cloud NDB up
is in the module 2a folder

00:04:50.973 --> 00:04:52.390
while the Python
3 Cloud Datastore

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.700
app is in the module 3b folder.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.070
Let's pause for a moment
for you to catch up and grab

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.990
your starting codebase.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:04:59.770
Got it?

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:00.400
Great.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.620
Now let's go containerize them.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.530
As with most migrations,
it's always a good idea

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.420
to check that you're
starting from a working

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.690
app, whether the module
2 Cloud NDB Python 2

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.810
app or the module 3 Cloud
Datastore Python 3 app,

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.250
please redeploy yours--

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.760
or ours, as suggested moments
ago-- with gcloud app deploy,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.340
and verify it works where
you get the same output

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.500
as from previous modules.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.720
Changing products from
App Engine to Cloud Run

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.120
requires configuration
changes, so we

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.270
suggest you make a full copy
of your working app folder.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.490
Now it's safe to make
the necessary changes

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.230
containerizing that app.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:40.850
First, delete the app.yaml file
that is not used in Cloud Run.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:44.855
If migrating the Python 2 Cloud
NDB app, also delete App Engine

00:05:44.855 --> 00:05:48.860
config.py and the lib folder,
more App Engine artifacts

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.510
unused in Cloud Run.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:53.660
Next let's tackle
requirements.txt.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:57.170
App Engine and Cloud Functions
are an source-based instructor

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:58.910
app with a
production-ready server.

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.960
Since Cloud Run is a bit more
DIY, you need to roll your own.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.410
Python Engine uses the gunicorn
production-ready server

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.980
if you don't specify your own.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.040
So let's use that
one for Cloud Run

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.770
by adding it to
requirements.txt.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.860
I like to put my packages in
lowest- to highest-level order,

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.690
which is why I listed it first.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:21.170
For the Python 2 app,
you'll have gunicorn, flask,

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.900
and cloud-ndb, whereas
for the Python 3 app,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.230
it'll be gunicorn, flask
and cloud-datastore.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.060
Version numbers are
a good practice.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.080
However, they change a lot and
differ between Python 2 and 3.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.210
So that's why we're
not showing any here.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.770
They're in the
repo files though.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.700
When using Docker,
the main config

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.730
file your need is a Docker file.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.490
So create that with the
content you see on screen

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.410
or download it from the repo.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.060
If you're new to
Docker files, you

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.280
need to start with a base
container, in this case

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.500
a minimal Python 2 or 3 image.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.710
From here, create and cd
into your working directory,

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:59.040
arbitrarily called "app,"
with the WORKDIR command.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.960
Now copy all the
source code into app

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.510
and run pip install so
your application can

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.900
use those packages listed
in requirements.txt.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.970
Finally, ENTRYPOINT
tells the system

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.400
to start gunicorn
to serve your app.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.460
Check the Dockerfile best
practices page for more info.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.520
Another good practice is
to add a dockerignore file.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.400
While it is optional, it
helps keep unnecessary files

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.200
from being added
to your container.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.120
It's less important
for our sample apps

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.940
since they're so small, but
it can have a positive impact

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.160
on your apps, controlling
container size

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.250
and preventing accidental
exposure of secrets like API

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.080
keys or OAuth credentials.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.880
This isn't
Python-related, so you

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.230
can use the same dockerignore
for both Python 2 and 3 apps.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.720
Since we're migrating
identical apps to Cloud Run,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.380
there are no other changes other
than configuration updates.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:48.090
That means we can
just deploy it now.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.290
We're done.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.750
Instead of gcloud app
deploy for App Engine,

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.990
the command now is gcloud
run deploy SVC_NAME,

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:58.830
dash-dash-source, dot to
deploy the app from the source

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.900
code in the current directory.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.540
Instead of deploying
an app, with Cloud Run,

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.200
you deploy a service.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.560
So you have to come up with
a service name, hopefully

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.410
more exciting than App
Engine's "default."

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.390
Cloud Run also has more options.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.850
So you're going to be asked
what region to deploy to

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.580
and whether you're allowing
open internet traffic.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.700
Lastly, Cloud Run is available
in other form factors,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.130
but the one closest to App
Engine is fully managed.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.000
If you don't want to get asked
all of these every single time,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.730
provide them on the command
line as options, like this one

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:33.720
here that I'm using for our app,
calling the service visitme.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.150
And like your App
Engine app, your service

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.610
should be available
globally in under a minute

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.850
at a unique URL you can point
to with the custom domain.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.039
Hit it with your browser
and confirm you now

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.690
have a running Cloud Run app.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:46.620
Cool.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.220
So what do you think, Martin?

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:51.740
MARTIN: Wow, Wes, that
was way easier than I

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.090
thought it was going to be.

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:56.070
I was a bit apprehensive about
learning these containers,

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:58.400
but it doesn't look that bad.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.040
But watching you
do it is too easy.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.248
Where can I try these
migrations myself?

00:09:04.248 --> 00:09:05.540
WESLEY CHUN: Of course, Martin.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.790
All modules have a
corresponding codelab

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:09.770
linked in the repo
and below, where

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.360
we walk through this
migration step by step.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.040
If things don't go well
migrating the Python 2 Cloud

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.260
NDB app, grab it again from
the module 2a Start folder.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:21.020
The same is true with the
Python 3 Cloud Datastore app.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.990
Reset to its module 3b
folder if you need to.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.960
You can compare your
solution with ours

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.120
in the module 4a or
4b Finish folders.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.870
Another link we're leaving
for you is to the gcloud run

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.030
deploy docs in case you're
curious how it works

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:36.570
and what else you
can do with it.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.280
MARTIN: Great.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.590
I'm looking forward to
doing those by hand.

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:42.680
But I am still concerned
about that Dockerfile.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.860
Our sample app is quite simple,
so there wasn't any trouble.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.627
But you know, out
in the real world--

00:09:47.627 --> 00:09:48.710
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, I know.

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:50.540
You have many more
dependencies and it's

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.322
easier to create suboptimal or
overly complex Docker files.

00:09:54.322 --> 00:09:56.030
These can lead to
slowing down the build,

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:59.180
deployment, and execution
of your containerized app.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.190
That's why you should
check out module 5 where

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.650
you don't have to worry
about Dockerfiles anymore.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.820
We'll add a link to the
video once it's ready.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.390
But for now, you can
already do its codelab.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.760
MARTIN: Oh, I look
forward to seeing that.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.250
By the way, my DevOps
coworkers told me

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:17.840
they prefer it if my app can be
integrated in a CI/CD pipeline.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.760
Can Cloud Run do that, Wes?

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:20.690
WESLEY CHUN: Oh yeah.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.300
On the CD side, one cool
feature of Cloud Run

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:25.910
is that you can deploy
direct from a code push.

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:27.590
This is configured
in the Cloud Console

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:30.830
if your code is in a repo
system like GitHub or Bitbucket.

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.200
When a commit takes place,
it triggers Cloud Build

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:34.880
to build and register
your container,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.590
then deploys it to Cloud Run.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.720
If you slip in a service
like GitHub actions

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.870
that builds and tests your code
before sending it to Cloud Run,

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.720
you'll have that CI/CD
environment you asked for.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:46.770
See the link to our
community tutorial

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:49.550
to learn more about
how to do that.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:50.660
MARTIN: OK, great.

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:51.710
Thanks, Wes.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.990
Aside from containerizing
my App Engine apps

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:56.480
for Cloud Run, what are
some other migrations I

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.040
should consider?

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.050
WESLEY CHUN: Well, aside
from the next video

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:03.080
on containerizing your app
without Docker, if you're

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:06.200
on Cloud NDB and have other
code using the Cloud Datastore

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.240
client library,
consider consolidating

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:09.830
to one client library.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.600
That's covered in module 3.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.400
If you're excited about
all those Firebase

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.410
features in Cloud Firestore
and willing to move

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.920
your app to a new project,
check out modules 6 and 10.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.340
If your app uses App Engine task
cues, migrate to Cloud Tasks.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:25.070
And that's covered in
modules 7, 8, and 9.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:27.380
Finally, if your App
Engine app is pretty small

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.890
or does only one thing, consider
migrating to Cloud Functions.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.870
That'll be found in module 11.

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:35.930
But note you have to port your
code from Python 2 to 3 first,

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.540
as Cloud Functions
only supports Python 3.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.880
And thank all of you for
staying with us on all

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.440
of these migration journeys.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.153
Please join us for the
module 5 migration station,

00:11:45.153 --> 00:11:46.820
not only to learn
more about containers,

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:49.028
but it's all of the stuff
that we didn't get a chance

00:11:49.028 --> 00:11:49.857
to cover today.

00:11:49.857 --> 00:11:51.440
This is Wesley Chun
from Google Cloud.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:52.970
On behalf of Martin
and Porter, we

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:54.920
hope to see you at the
next Migration Station

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.450
or on another Serverless
Expedition soon.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:01.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

